125, 0.0014467239379882812, -0.004306793212890625, -0.04705810546875, -0.00933074951171875, 0.0211029052734375, -0.051483154296875, 0.0328369140625, -0.0276336669921875, -0.036163330078125, -0.00516510009765625, 0.00893402099609375, 0.00177764892578125, -0.0151214599609375, -0.0144195556640625, -0.0170135498046875, -0.0039520263671875, -0.0263671875, -0.0006031990051269531, 0.0262603759765625, -0.00923919677734375, 0.032135009765625, -0.0093994140625, 0.06011962890625, -0.0002827644348144531, -0.03363037109375, 0.01085662841796875, -0.047393798828125, 0.034698486328125, -0.0009489059448242188, -0.0152130126953125, 0.012237548828125, 0.007781982421875, -0.001834869384765625, -0.0147247314453125, 0.02789306640625, -0.0146484375, 0.00365447998046875, 0.00960540771484375, -0.032379150390625, -0.06817626953125, 0.0122528076171875, 0.01088714599609375, 0.01497650146484375, -0.033172607421875, 0.024871826171875, -0.05462646484375, 0.0125885009765625, -0.03436279296875, -0.00899505615234375, -0.0236358642578125, -0.018646240234375, 0.0086212158203125, 0.0163421630859375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Black holes are among the most fascinating and extreme objects studied in astronomy and astrophysics. A black hole is an astronomical body so compact that its gravity prevents anything, including light, from escaping. In the framework of Albert Einstein's theory of general relativity\u2014which describes gravitation as the bending or curvature of spacetime caused by mass and energy\u2014if a mass is compressed within a certain critical radius, it will form a black hole.\n\nThis critical radius is known as the Schwarzschild radius, and it specifically applies to non-rotating, uncharged black holes (called Schwarzschild black holes). Other types of black holes include rotating (Kerr) black holes, which possess angular momentum, and charged (Reissner-Nordstr\u00f6m) black holes, which carry electric charge. Rotation affects the structure of the black hole and leads to phenomena such as frame dragging, while charge alters the spacetime geometry and the nature of the event horizon.\n\nThe boundary beyond which nothing can escape a black hole is called the event horizon. The event horizon is a well-defined physical boundary in spacetime: it marks the point of no return, beyond which events cannot influence an outside observer. For an object falling into a black hole, crossing the event horizon produces no noticeable change locally; the object would not detect anything unusual at that precise moment. However, to a distant observer, the object appears to slow down and never quite crosses the horizon. The strength of tidal forces\u2014differences in gravitational pull\u2014near the event horizon depends on the black hole's mass. For stellar-mass black holes, the event horizon is relatively small, so the gradient of gravity is steep and tidal forces are extreme. In contrast, for supermassive black holes, the event horizon is much larger, resulting in a gentler gradient and relatively mild tidal forces at the horizon.\n\nGeneral relativity also predicts that every black hole should contain a central singularity, a point where the curvature of spacetime becomes infinite and the known laws of physics break down. However, it is possible that quantum effects, such as those described by candidate quantum gravity theories like loop quantum gravity or string theory, could modify or resolve this singularity. Currently, there is no consensus or experimental evidence for any particular quantum gravity theory. For example, Hawking radiation\u2014a quantum mechanical process predicted by Stephen Hawking\u2014suggests that black holes can emit radiation and may eventually evaporate over extremely long timescales, far exceeding the current age of the universe for stellar or larger black holes. However, Hawking radiation has not yet been directly observed.\n\nBlack holes serve as important laboratories for testing our understanding of gravity, quantum physics, and the fundamental laws of the universe. Observations of gravitational waves from black hole mergers provide strong, though indirect, evidence for their existence, while studies of accretion disks and the motion of stars near black holes further support their presence. More recently, the Event Horizon Telescope has produced direct images of the shadow of a supermassive black hole, offering direct visual evidence of event horizons and black hole properties.\n\nIn summary, black holes remain at the frontier of physics and astronomy, raising open questions about the nature of singularities, the unification of quantum mechanics and gravity, and the ultimate fate of information falling into black holes. Ongoing and future observations, as well as advances in theoretical physics, promise to deepen our understanding of these enigmatic objects."}}